Subject: A constructor wrapper called with a fresh this runs the function it applies, without its own frame

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_wrap_init` takes a construct site that the generator recognised as calling a constructor wrapper
    (`function () { this.X.apply(this, arguments); }`, by its register program) and runs `this.X` with the same arguments directly, in
    place of the wrapper: no wrapper frame, no `arguments` object, no polymorphic read of `this.X`, no trip through the `apply` builtin.
  - The site keeps a cache (eight words in the generated unit): the shape of the fresh `this`, the function `X` it resolves to, `X`'s
    shape and the epoch. It hits while `aot_pic_epoch` is unchanged and `X` keeps its shape. It fills only for a plain data property
    found on ordinary prototypes (no accessor, proxy or exotic object), holding an ordinary bytecode function whose prototype is its
    realm's Function.prototype, has no own `apply`, and whose Function.prototype.apply is the builtin.
  - Anything else returns JS_UNINITIALIZED with nothing done, and the generated code runs the wrapper as before.
  - Kit builds only (`QJS_KIT_TEST_KNOBS`): `QJS_AOT_WCSTALE` makes a filled cache never invalidate, the negative control for the tests.
  - Kit builds only: `QJS_AOT_WCABA` prints a line each time a call finds the cached shape pointer, the same pic epoch and a different shape-free epoch (the
    state in which an address-only compare would have hit a freed and reused shape).

Why we need it
  Every class built by Prototype's `Class.create` shares one wrapper bytecode, so its `this.initialize` read is megamorphic and its
  `apply(this, arguments)` materialises an `arguments` object, enters the builtin and calls through the generic call path. Measured on
  raytrace, the wrapper and what it reaches are about a fifth of the instructions; a program whose classes are their own initialize
  runs 25% fewer.

Soundness
  The result is what the wrapper returns (undefined) or the exception from the call. The observable differences are limited to the
  wrapper's own frame: it no longer appears in a stack trace taken inside X. `arguments.length` inside X is the count passed, as through
  apply. A `this.X` that could run code (getter, proxy) or find `X` through anything but ordinary prototype data properties is refused.
  The cache is exactly as strong as the generator's other epoch-trusting caches: every write into a prototype slot, every addition to or
  deletion from a prototype, every prototype link change and every cache cell reset moves `aot_pic_epoch`, and X's own shape is compared
  on every hit, so an own `apply` added to X (which is not a prototype and moves no epoch) is seen. A call with fewer arguments than X
  declares, or to an X without a native entry, goes through JS_Call, as apply would.

Why the cache holds no references (and what keeps its raw pointers honest)
  The cache stores raw pointers (the receiver shape, X, X's shape). A shape that is freed can have its address reused by another shape, which a pointer
  compare cannot tell from the cached one, and X is then dangling. This is reachable without any prototype write: `C.prototype = Q` writes an ordinary
  function's property and moves no epoch; the old instances die; the old prototype's shape is freed; the next shape lands at its address. It happens in
  raytrace itself (136 times in one run, counted by the kit-only detector below). The test therefore also compares `rt->shape_free_epoch`, which moves
  whenever any shape is freed, BEFORE it dereferences anything cached: while it is unchanged the cached shape is alive, so are its prototype chain and X (X
  can only be dropped by a write or delete on a prototype, which moves `aot_pic_epoch`), and the compare of the shape pointer is a compare of live objects.
  Chosen over holding a counted reference on the shape because it needs no teardown: the cache lives in the generated unit's static data, shared by every
  runtime of the process, and a reference there would have to be released at each `JS_FreeRuntime` and at unit unload. The price is a refill whenever a shape
  anywhere is freed, measured on raytrace at +0.5% C.Minstr (1448.7 -> 1456.0). The wrapper bytecode pointer is also raw; it cannot matter: the site reaches
  this helper only after testing that the callee's bound entry is the one generated for this wrapper's digest, so a reused address holding another wrapper
  fails that test, and one holding a function of the same digest has the same code and therefore the same atoms.

Assumptions
  The cache (`wc`) is static data of the generated unit, like the existing `e_vg`/`e_vm` statics: it is process-global. Two runtimes in one process share it
  (a hit needs the cached shape pointer to equal a live shape of the running runtime, which another runtime's shape cannot be, and the epochs are read from
  the running runtime, so the sharing only costs refills), and two runtimes running on different threads would race on its multi-word fill. The statics
  design already assumes one runtime runs compiled code at a time.

Behaviour changes
  - The wrapper's own frame no longer appears in a stack trace (or in a debugger) taken inside X.
  - Nothing else observable: the call, the arguments, `this`, the exceptions and the result are those of the wrapper.

Functions touched
  `qjs_aot_wrap_init` (new), declared in quickjs-aot-rt.h.

No.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
